# Question 07 : 
    Explain the difference between position: absolute; and position: fixed; in CSS.

# Answer 07 : 
    Difference Between position: absolute and position: fixed in CSS

    Both absolute and fixed are CSS positioning properties used to place elements at a specific position on a webpage, but they behave differently.

    1. position: absolute

    When an element has position: absolute, it is positioned relative to its nearest positioned parent element (a parent with position: relative, absolute, or fixed).
    If there is no positioned parent, it will be positioned relative to the entire webpage.

    Example : 

        .box {
            position: absolute;
            top: 50px;
            left: 100px;
        }

    2. position: fixed : 

        When an element has position: fixed, it is positioned relative to the browser window (viewport).
        It stays in the same position even when the page is scrolled.

        Example : 

            .box {
                position: fixed;
                bottom: 10px;
                right: 10px;
            }

